  count-claims    count the FOOTNOTED declarative claims in blue's report (read-only)
  fetch           cached, hash-verified web read (replaces WebFetch); serves both sides the same bytes
  friction        a capability gap or protocol misfit, as an event that survives aborts: --reason. CLOSE THIS CHANNEL EVERY SITTING: --none --reason "<what you reached for and found>" says nothing blocked you, which silence cannot say — an empty friction log reads the same whether the sitting was clean or the channel went unused
  help            Help about any command
  inquiry-support your per-round verdict that the REPORT still carries a line of inquiry: --id Q1 --as supported|weakened|unsupported|absent --reason "<what the report says for it, quoted>". Read the report ONCE this round and vote every line against that read
  mint            mint a board gap (id is TOOL-assigned; --key <stable-label> makes retries idempotent): --class <slug> --quote "<the exact sentence it lives at>" --problem "..."|--reason --fix "..." --check "<acceptance check red runs at re-audit>" --severity/--likelihood/--impact/--complexity <grade> [--supersedes R1-2,R1-7] [--found-by L5-F3,L6-F2]
  motion          file and rule on a motion — a grade dispute, a petition, or a ruling on a direction. One mechanism, one id.
  near-match      screen a candidate against the board before minting: --problem "<what is wrong>" [--quote "<the sentence it lives at>"] — returns the top 5 gaps (open AND closed) by lexical overlap, so a near-duplicate surfaces as a reopen (mint --supersedes <id>) rather than a fresh gap. The tool SCREENS and ranks; you decide reopen-or-new. Read-only: it records nothing.
  position        the round's ### RED section (prose via --reason)
